# Third-party notices

The MIT grant above covers everything in this repository *except* the
third-party material described below. In short:

- The port's own source, tools, tests and generator are MIT outright.
- The generated tables under `src/generated/` and the two generated grammars
  under `syntaxes/` are MIT as to their arrangement, but their contents are
  third-party: they carry the game's names and signatures, argument types from
  the 100.022 reference, the game's own per-object instance-variable lists,
  and documentation prose from the sources described below. So do the hand
  tables `src/tables/buttons.ts` and `src/tables/custom-objects.ts`, whose
  text is worded from those docs, and `src/tables/ntt-names.ts`, a list of
  name prefixes assembled from them.
- `data/gml-configuration.json` descends from the original extension, as
  described below.
- The vendored dumps and documentation under `api/` and the community
  reference under `docs/` are not covered at all, and are included here under
  no license of their own.
- `resources/icon.png` is Vlambeer's Nuclear Throne icon, not the port's own
  work; see "Nuclear Throne icon" below.

## The extension this repository grew out of

This repository began in 2020 as "Rivals of Aether GML Support" by fudgepop01,
a VS Code extension for a different game's GML dialect. That extension was
published without a license file; no such file has ever existed in this
repository's history. All of its code was removed in commits `3006ae3` and
`ca12c65` and survives only in the git history, which is left intact for
attribution.

Two things in the working tree still descend from it rather than being written
from scratch. `data/gml-configuration.json` has continuous history back to
fudgepop01's first commit `2da3799` (`git log --follow` shows `2da3799`,
`8b080ff`, `3006ae3`); it has only been edited in small ways since. The shape
of the `package.json` extension manifest descends from the same commit.
Because the original was unlicensed, the MIT grant above is offered only for
the Nuclear Throne Together work, and cannot and does not purport to license
anything that originates before those commits.

## Nuclear Throne Together documentation (YellowAfterlife)

`api/ntt-docs/` and `api/ntt-100.022-reference/` are copied from
YellowAfterlife's `YAL-Game-Tools/bits-of-nuclear-throne` repository
(https://github.com/YAL-Game-Tools/bits-of-nuclear-throne). That repository
has no license file and GitHub reports no license for it; its README says
"All rights reserved and such." It is vendored here for reference and for
generating documentation strings, under no grant of permission that its author
has given.

Material derived from those sources is redistributed in the packaged
extension, in more than one file:

- `out/generated/docs.js` carries the documentation prose itself, generated by
  `tools/` from the DocMark sources in `api/ntt-docs/scripting/` into
  `src/generated/docs.ts`.
- `out/generated/functions.js` carries argument types that exist only in
  `api/ntt-100.022-reference/api.gml` — 252 of them, on 138 functions.
- `out/generated/variables.js` carries the 57 built-in instance variables that
  come from that reference's `default.gml` (of 90 variables in the table).
- `out/tables/buttons.js` and `out/tables/custom-objects.js` are hand-written,
  but their docstrings are worded from `api/ntt-docs/scripting/NTT-Input.dmd`
  and their field lists come from `api/ntt-docs/ref/Custom*.gml`; some of that
  wording is close to verbatim.
- `out/generated/object-fields.js` carries instance-variable types and prose
  taken from the six object pages in `api/ntt-docs/objects/` — 226 of its
  1,493 fields are known only to those pages, and a further 191 that the
  game's own dump names get their type or their description from them. The
  descriptions are the pages' wording, shortened.
- `api/fields-overrides.gml` is one hand-written entry line, adding three
  fields to `UberCont`, under two notes that quote and paraphrase
  `api/ntt-docs/Changelog.md`.

(The generator also writes `src/generated/docs.json`, which is a build
artifact for inspection: nothing imports it and `.vscodeignore` keeps all of
`src/` out of the `.vsix`.) Anyone redistributing this extension, or reusing
those strings and types, should treat them as YellowAfterlife's work.

## Nuclear Throne Together API dump

`api/ntt-100.034/` and `api/ntt-fields-2025-07-16/` are machine-generated
dumps produced by Nuclear Throne Together itself (the in-game `/gmlapi`
command); the second is the per-object instance-variable list, kept under its
own header date because the game no longer regenerates it. The names,
signatures, argument types and field lists in them are the game's own output
and are likewise third-party material outside the MIT grant above; Nuclear
Throne is by Vlambeer and Nuclear Throne Together is by YellowAfterlife. The
dumps are vendored so the extension can be rebuilt without a copy of the game.

Every generated table in the `.vsix` — `out/generated/functions.js`,
`constants.js`, `variables.js`, `assets.js`, `object-fields.js`, `meta.js` —
and both grammars in `syntaxes/` are built from those dumps and so reproduce
the game's own names and signatures.

## Nuclear Throne icon (Vlambeer)

`resources/icon.png`, the icon shown in the Extensions view and in the
`.vsix`, is the 256x256 icon resource extracted verbatim from
`nuclearthrone.exe` by `tools/extract-icon.ps1`. It is Vlambeer's copyrighted
artwork for Nuclear Throne, used here to identify the game this extension
supports; no permission for it has been sought or given, and it is not covered
by the MIT grant above. Anyone redistributing this extension should treat the
icon as Vlambeer's and replace it if that use is not acceptable to them.

